add(101,"Provided he does not bowl more than two overs in succession, a bowler may change ends twice but no more often in an innings.","14","109","May 4th 1870",page=81)
add(102,"The pitch may be rolled for 10 minutes before the start of play. This change is interesting since it emanated from the County Secretaries, a reflection of the increasing prestige of County cricket. Although the change was decided on in 1883, it was not promulgated until the publication of the new code in the following year.","98","126","May 2nd 1883",bold="This change is interesting since it emanated from the County Secretaries, a reflection of the increasing prestige of County cricket. Although the change was decided on in 1883, it was not promulgated until the publication of the new code in the following year.",page=81)
add("","The 1884 Code: the preparation of this Code has been fully described in Chapter 4, from which it will be clear that its introduction involved no major changes in the conduct of the game.",page=81,row_type="heading")
d84="April 21st 1884"
add("","For the first time reference is found to the number of players on each side, to the number of innings, to the method by which runs are scored and the results of matches assessed, to the appointment of umpires and scorers,","1","",d84,sub="u1a",page=81)
add("","and throughout the revision free use is made of the term “Dead ball” about which previously there were no very explicit instructions.","49","112",d84,sub="u1b",page=81)
add("","The thickness of the bail is specified for the first time to remove doubts about the overall height of the wicket.","83","125",d84,sub="u2",page=81)
add("","“Boundaries” are officially mentioned for the first time, though they were in use many years earlier.","13","",d84,sub="u3",page=81)
add("","A last-minute addition was made to the “No ball” Law to make it clear that the umpire must call if not absolutely satisfied of the fairness of a delivery.","100","113",d84,sub="u4",page=81)
add("","Three Laws dealing with one-day cricket were removed from the general code and shown separately.","","",d84,sub="u5",page=82)
add("","It is interesting to note that the opportunity was not taken to revive the 1863 proposal to amend the “L.B.W.” Law.","99","128",d84,sub="u6",page=82)
add("","All the changes mentioned above in no way altered the intent of the earlier Laws, and the only important alterations are as follows:",date=d84,page=82,row_type="note")
add(103,"A striker becomes liable to be out for any infringement of the Laws by his runner — previously only his liability in respect of a run out had been mentioned.","94","129",d84,page=82)
add(104,"The umpires are to be appointed “one for each end”—a truly significant phrase.","92","",d84,page=82)
add(105,"Although the term “Bye” is found much earlier in the Laws for Single-wicket, and clearly both Byes and Leg Byes were a recognised means of scoring, they are now specifically defined for the first time.","56","",d84,page=82)
add(106,"The Law defining a wicket being “down” confirms that if one bail is off, the fall of the remaining bail suffices without the need to strike a stump from the ground.","85","135",d84,page=82)
add(107,"The “Obstruction” Law is extended to cover not only the obstruction of a catch but now any form of such tactics by either batsman; except in the former case either batsman is liable to be dismissed.","38","",d84,page=82)
add(108,"The Over is increased from four balls to five.","14","116","May 1st 1889",page=83)
add(109,"A bowler is permitted to change ends as often as he pleases, but may not bowl two overs in succession in any innings.","101","","May 1st 1889",page=83)
add(110,"The batting side is allowed to declare its innings closed at any time on the last day of a match.","","115","May 1st 1889",page=83)
add(111,"The limit of runs for a “follow on” is increased to 120 runs in a three-day match. The Law still however provides no option in its application.","95","114","June 10th 1894",page=83)
add(112,"The ball becomes “dead” if it lodges in the batsman's clothing.","1884","1947","May 3rd 1899",page=83)
add(113,"Either umpire may call “No ball” if not satisfied with the absolute fairness of any delivery.","1884","133","Oct. 30th 1899",page=83)
add(114,"The enforcement of a “Follow on” becomes optional for the side in the lead. The limits are altered to: for a three-day match 150 runs, for a two-day match 100 runs, and for a one-day match 75 runs.","111","121","May 2nd 1900",page=83)
add(115,"A declaration is allowed in a three-day match after the start of the luncheon interval on the second day.","110","118","May 2nd 1900",page=83)
add(116,"The Over is increased from five balls to six.","108","131","May 2nd 1900",page=83)
add(117,"The Bowling Crease is lengthened to 8 feet 8 inches.","69","","May 7th 1902",page=83)
add(118,"In a two-day match a declaration on the first day is allowed provided 100 minutes remain for play.","115","119","May 2nd 1906",page=83)
add(119,"A declaration is allowed at any time on the second day of a three-day match.","118","121","May 4th 1910",page=83)
add(120,"The striker cannot be out stumped off a “No ball,” but may be run out—this change was destined to cause much difficulty in interpretation.","54","136","May 1st 1912",page=84)  # starts p.83, ends p.84
add(121,"If the start of play is delayed by weather, the “follow on” and “declaration” rules are to apply in accordance with the number of days remaining after the actual start of play.","114; 119","123; 124","May 6th 1914 and May 7th 1919",page=84)
add(122,"The ball is to measure between 8 13/16 and 9 inches in circumference.","82","130","May 4th 1927",page=84)
add(123,"The limit of runs for a “follow on” in a three- or more day match is altered to 150 runs.","121","","May 4th 1927",page=84)
add(124,"Serial Number 119 is extended to apply in a match of more than three days.","121","","May 4th 1927",page=84)
add(125,"The height of the wicket is increased to be not less than 27 inches nor more than 28 inches—in the latter case the width to be 9 instead of 8 inches.","1884","","May 6th 1931",page=84)
add(126,"The time allowed for sweeping and rolling the pitch before the start of each day's play and between the innings is reduced to 7 minutes.","102","","May 6th 1931",page=84)
