add("","The 1947 Code: since the reasons leading up to the revision of the Laws in 1947 have been fully explained in Chapter 5, no special further introduction is now required. Apart from the definite alterations set out later the most important features are as follows:",page=84,row_type="heading")
d47="May 7th 1947"
add("","A definition of the term “Pitch” is included, and the use of the term “Wicket” is confined to the stumps and the bails.","","",d47,sub="u1",page=85)
add("","The term “Special Regulation” is defined in order to provide for any special playing conditions agreed upon as necessary to meet the requirements of differing climates, competitive cricket and so on.","","",d47,sub="u2",page=85)
add("","A new Law is included to collect and expand all the many references to the ball becoming “dead” previously scattered throughout the 1884 code.","1884","132",d47,sub="u3",page=85)
add("","The “L.B.W.” Law is more closely defined in respect of a ball hitting the striker either full-pitch or above the level of the bails.","128","",d47,sub="u4",page=85)
add("","The umpires are no longer required to pitch the wickets, but are to satisfy themselves that they have been properly pitched.","63","",d47,sub="u5",page=85)
add(129,"The batting captain has the right to say where a fielding substitute may not field; previously he was required to say where he should field. N.B. Subject to consent a substitute may keep wicket.","103","",d47,page=85)
add(130,"The provision of a new ball at the start of an innings is no longer obligatory in every class of cricket.","47; 122","",d47,page=85)
add(131,"A major change is made in respect of the final over of a match. Previously the batting side alone had the option of continuing play if a wicket fell within two minutes of “Time,” but now the last over of any match must be finished if either captain so demands.","","",d47,sub="a",page=85)
add(131,"Latitude is provided for the “Over” to be of either 6 or 8 balls in order to meet the requirements of cricket overseas.","116","",d47,sub="b",page=86)
add(132,"The new “Dead ball” Law for the first time states when the ball ceases to be “dead.”","1884","",d47,page=86)
add(133,"The “No ball” Law no longer requires the bowler to have his back foot “grounded” at the instant of delivery, though it must still be behind the line of the Bowling crease. Previously umpires had found it impossible to adjudicate on this point, and as a matter of course had disregarded the old instruction.","113","",d47,page=86)
add(134,"It is made clear that the striker cannot create a “Wide” by stepping back.","91","",d47,page=86)
add(135,"Whereas before it had been ruled that the least disturbance of a bail sufficed for the wicket to be “down,” it is now necessary for the bail to fall clear of the grooves on the stumps.","106","",d47,page=86)
add(136,"The Laws regarding “Running out” and “Stumping” revert to the original eighteenth-century conception, in order to eliminate many difficulties of interpretation which previously existed. It is now ruled that when the wicket-keeper puts down the wicket, the striker is not to be given “Run out” unless in the opinion of the umpire he is attempting a run.","120","",d47,page=86)
add(137,"The old Laws were obscure on the point of the wicket-keeper taking the ball in front of the wicket. The Law now states that he must remain behind the wicket until the striker plays the ball, or the ball passes the wicket, or the striker attempts a run.","","",d47,page=87)  # starts p.86, ends p.87
add(138,"Previously no mention was made to the incommoding of the striker by any fieldsman except the wicket-keeper. This is now corrected by bringing obstruction of this nature within the judgment of the umpires in respect of unfair play.","21","",d47,page=87)
